Using only WIKI_PATH=/root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki, the wiki supports a detailed description of Hermes Agent, but it contains no pages or mentions for OpenClaw, Open Claw, AutoGPT, Auto-GPT, or Auto GPT. So a factual feature-by-feature comparison is only possible on the Hermes side; the OpenClaw and AutoGPT columns must be “not in wiki.”

Comparison:

| Dimension | Hermes Agent | OpenClaw | AutoGPT |
|---|---|---|---|
| Basic identity | Open-source MIT self-improving AI agent built by Nous Research | Not in wiki | Not in wiki |
| Core differentiator | Built-in learning loop: turns task experience into reusable skills, improves them, and builds a user model across sessions | Not in wiki | Not in wiki |
| Procedural memory | Uses a skills system: markdown `SKILL.md` files with YAML frontmatter, stored under `~/.hermes/skills/`, with `/learn`, auto-save, hand-written skills, and Skills Hub installation | Not in wiki | Not in wiki |
| Long-term/context memory | Agent-curated memory, cross-session recall with FTS5 + LLM summarization, and user preference/decision-history modeling | Not in wiki | Not in wiki |
| Deployment | Runs on 6 backends: local, Docker, SSH, Daytona, Singularity, Modal | Not in wiki | Not in wiki |
| Model/provider flexibility | Model-agnostic; switch providers with `hermes model`; supports Nous Portal, OpenRouter, OpenAI, custom endpoints, and examples including local Ollama | Not in wiki | Not in wiki |
| User access / interface | 20+ chat platforms via one messaging gateway, including Telegram, Discord, Slack, WhatsApp, Signal, Email, Microsoft Teams, and CLI | Not in wiki | Not in wiki |
| Automation / agent orchestration | Wiki says Hermes includes built-in cron scheduler, isolated subagents for parallel work, and MCP support | Not in wiki | Not in wiki |

Bottom line from the wiki:

Hermes Agent is documented in this wiki as a self-improving, persistent agent platform whose main distinction is the learning loop plus skills system: it can convert experience into reusable procedures, refine them, remember user preferences across sessions, run on multiple deployment backends, switch model providers, and connect through many messaging platforms.

The wiki does not contain enough information to compare OpenClaw or AutoGPT on architecture, memory, deployment, licensing, model routing, or user interfaces. Under the wiki’s own guardrail — “Cite or say ‘not in the wiki’” — those details should not be inferred.

